WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.728
Checkout charges 40 euros through the payment service, which asks the card issuer.

2
00:00:07.728 --> 00:00:10.180
Each arrow is a call to another machine.

3
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:15.643
A local function call takes about a nanosecond, and it either returns or throws.

4
00:00:15.643 --> 00:00:22.080
A call to another machine takes hundreds of microseconds or more, and it can also end in silence.

5
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:29.868
So checkout gives the call a deadline, 300 milliseconds, and gRPC sends it along as time remaining.

6
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:37.988
Payments spends 120 milliseconds on its own work, then calls the issuer, which needs 500 milliseconds today.

7
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.964
At 300, checkout's deadline runs out, and it gets DEADLINE_EXCEEDED.

8
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:52.005
If the issuer's call carried no deadline, it would work on for another 320 milliseconds, for an answer nobody is waiting for.

9
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.359
In Java and Go, gRPC passes the deadline on instead.

10
00:00:56.359 --> 00:01:05.397
The issuer's call arrives with 180 milliseconds left, so at 300 everyone gives up together, and the issuer stops.

11
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:07.322
But was the card charged?

12
00:01:07.322 --> 00:01:12.940
From checkout, a lost request, a lost reply and a slow server all look the same.

13
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:16.655
Say the card was charged and only the reply was lost.

14
00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:22.171
Checkout retries, payments sees a brand new request, and the card is charged twice.

15
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:24.508
The fix is an idempotency key.

16
00:01:24.508 --> 00:01:31.179
Checkout makes one, k-7f, before the first attempt, and sends the same key with every retry.

17
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:36.835
Payments claims the key before doing any work, and stores the result under it.

18
00:01:36.835 --> 00:01:40.621
The retry finds k-7f already done, and gets ch_1 back.

19
00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:42.373
The card is charged once.

20
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:44.608
A remote call can end in silence.

21
00:01:44.608 --> 00:01:51.208
Bound it with a deadline that travels down the chain, and make asking again safe with an idempotency key.
